/T (Creation and the First Patriarchs In the beginning, according to the book of Genesis, the universe was created by a divine act, establishing the earth, the heavens, and all living beings. This account not only marks the beginning of the world but also establishes the foundation for the relationship between God and humanity. The first patriarchs, such as Adam, Noah, and Abraham, emerge as fundamental figures who shape the narrative of humanity's beginnings. Adam, created in the image of God, represents original innocence and the subsequent fall that led to the current human condition. Noah, in turn, symbolizes renewal and hope through the flood, a new beginning for humanity. Abraham, the father of nations, stands out for his unwavering faith and obedience, tracing a path of covenants and promises that resonate to this day.)
/E (Creation and the First Patriarchs In the beginning, according to the book of Genesis, the universe was created by a divine act, establishing the earth, the heavens, and all living beings. This account not only marks the beginning of the world but also establishes the foundation for the relationship between God and humanity. The first patriarchs, such as Adam, Noah, and Abraham, emerge as fundamental figures who shape the narrative of humanity's beginnings. Adam, created in the image of God, represents original innocence and the subsequent fall that led to the current human condition. Noah, in turn, symbolizes renewal and hope through the flood, a new beginning for humanity. Abraham, the father of nations, stands out for his unwavering faith and obedience, tracing a path of covenants and promises that resonate to this day.)
