/T (In the desert, on Mount Sinai, Moses received the Ten Commandments, a set of ethical and moral principles that would serve as the basis for the new covenant between God and his people. These commandments were not merely rules, but a call to a life of justice, compassion, and devotion. The covenant established at Sinai was a landmark in the history of the Israelites, reinforcing the idea that they were the chosen people, with a sacred commitment to follow divine teachings. This chapter of Exodus is not only about physical liberation, but about spiritual transformation and the formation of a community identity grounded in faith and obedience to God.)
