/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 70 0 R
/T (Church Expansion: The expansion of the Early Church was remarkable, breaking through cultural and geographical barriers. Initially centered in Jerusalem, the church began to spread throughout Judea, Samaria, and to the ends of the earth, as commanded by Jesus. This growth was driven not only by the fervent preaching of the apostles but also by the dedication of countless disciples who, moved by the Spirit, carried the message of Christ to new regions. The conversion of Saul of Tarsus, who became the apostle Paul, was a crucial turning point, as he became one of the church's greatest missionaries, establishing Christian communities in various cities of the Roman Empire. This period of expansion was characterized by challenges and conflicts, but also by a strong sense of community and purpose among the early Christians.)
/E (Church Expansion: The expansion of the Early Church was remarkable, breaking through cultural and geographical barriers. Initially centered in Jerusalem, the church began to spread throughout Judea, Samaria, and to the ends of the earth, as commanded by Jesus. This growth was driven not only by the fervent preaching of the apostles but also by the dedication of countless disciples who, moved by the Spirit, carried the message of Christ to new regions. The conversion of Saul of Tarsus, who became the apostle Paul, was a crucial turning point, as he became one of the church's greatest missionaries, establishing Christian communities in various cities of the Roman Empire. This period of expansion was characterized by challenges and conflicts, but also by a strong sense of community and purpose among the early Christians.)
